     "result_text_tail": "R renews in 2027, and enterprise caps limit the rest. If SMB customers react to a 12% rise the way they did to the 2023 increase, it falls to $5.0\u20136.3M, and Palisade loses about 320\u2013640 SMB customers.\n- **The Head of Customer Success's claim to \"halve SMB churn\" isn't supported.** Even if promotion customers behaved like everyone else, SMB churn would fall by about a third.\n- **The board dashboard's \"1.4% \u2192 2.3%\" can't be matched to the segment data.** The deck recommends defining that measure properly.\n\n**Two things the leadership team should know:**\n- **The 15% growth goal isn't met on these numbers alone.** If each segment keeps its last-12-month net revenue retention, the plan reaches about $98.3M (+14.2%). The last $0.7M needs revenue from new customers or the checkpoint upside, and the pack has no new-customer forecast.\n- **The CFO's plan shows more ARR on paper,** up to $2M more. The deck says so on slide 11 and explains why the lower-risk plan is still the better choice.\n\n**Choices I made where I would have asked you** (all listed on the last slide):\n- Mid-market is set at +10% because that is the only level that was tested, not the CFO's 12%.\n- Enterprise contracts without a cap are set at 10%.\n- The SMB test uses +8%, so results compare with the 2023 increase.\n- The checkpoint thresholds are my proposals for leadership to confirm.\n- All ARR figures are year-end 2027 annual rates, not revenue earned during the year.\n\nThe scripts that build the deck are in `./build/`."
    }
